   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the private housing finance law, in relation to increasing the\ntimeline for completion and amount spent on emergency home repairs for\nlow-moderate income senior citizens through the RESTORE program",
    "basePrintNo": "S9193",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S9193-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1262, Priv Hous Fin L",
    "lawSection": "Private Housing Finance Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9193\n\nSPONSOR: HINCHEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the private housing finance law, in relation to increas-\ning the timeline for completion and amount spent on emergency home\nrepairs for low-moderate income senior citizens through the RESTORE\nprogram\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of the bill is to create a more flexible timeline for\ncompleting repair projects and to increase both the amount that can be\nspent on each repair project and the percent set-aside for managing the\ngrant program.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: amends subdivision 1 of section 1262 of the private housing\nfinance law by removing five-day and fourteen-day intermediate deadlines\nand leaving completion of repairs at sixty days.  Subdivision 2 of\nsection 1262 is amended by replacing $10,000 with $20,000 as the maximum\nallowed per repair project. Subdivision 3 is amended by increasing seven\nand a half to ten percent of the grant set-aside for program management.\nSection 2: sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe RESIDENTIAL EMERGENCY SERVICES TO OFFER HOME REPAIRS TO THE ELDERLY\nPROGRAM, otherwise known as RESTORE, was established in 2018 to assist\nlow-moderate income senior citizens with the cost of emergency repairs\nto their homes, such as leaking roofs, non-functioning boilers, etc.\nThese repairs are provided by local non-profit housing organizations,\nlike rural preservation companies, who hire local contractors to perform\nthe work. The cost of these repairs, both in terms of labor and materi-\nals, has increased substantially since 2018 and particularly since the\npandemic. Not only have the costs increased, but the timeline to identi-\nfy capable contractors to visit the homes to estimate the work needed,\norder the materials, and return to do the repair work has also increased\ndramatically. The Legislature in the 2022 state Budget increased funding\nfor RESTORE by $2 million in recognition of the importance of this\nprogram to keep seniors in their homes and aging in place. These\nproposed changes would align the implementation of this worthy program\nwith current labor conditions, supply chain issues, and additional fund-\ning.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
